  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong westerly winds on Tuesday could lead to \nsome isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nIncreasing confidence on Tuesday that, as the low pressure \nsystem deepens in the Northern Maritimes, we can expect strong \nsurface high pressure to dive over the eastern Great Lakes. This\nwill lead to a strong pressure gradient between the low and \nhigh, resulting in gusty west winds developing during the \nafternoon and evening hours on Monday. Tuesday probabilities for\n35+ mph wind gusts have stayed consistent to 70-90%, with 40+ \nmph probabilities still 40-70% for Millinocket and areas north.\nFor the Bangor region and interior Downeast, 35+ mph wind gusts\nprobabilities are 20-40%. 40+ mph probabilities have decreased \nto less than 10%. There is ensemble guidance suggesting a \npotential of wind advisory headlines (31-39 mph sustained or \ngusts 45+ mph) for northern Aroostook. Uncertainty remains in \nwind speeds due to uncertainty with cloud cover throughout the \nday on Tuesday. However, it is likely that significant leaf loss\nmay be possible in areas of Northern Maine where peak foliage \nhas been reached. Additional leaf loss is possible in the \nCentral Highlands to Downeast Maine where the foliage is \nmoderate to high. Gusty winds could also lead to some isolated \npower outages. Continue to monitor the forecast for updates, as \nmodels get a better handle on the atmospheric set up.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain showers throughout the \nforecast area on Monday, with light rainfall totals and little \nimprovement to ongoing drought conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn occluding low pressure system will track across northern\nQuebec Monday through Tuesday. At the same time, a coastal low\nwill track up the Atlantic coast before tracking up the eastern\ncoast of Nova Scotia. There is increasing confidence that the\ntiming between these two systems will align such that they\ninteract with each other over our forecast area on Monday. The\ncoastal low will be the system that provides the moisture for\nrainfall, with PWATs 1.0-1.2 inches. Meanwhile, the northern low\nwill be comparatively dry with PWATs closer to 0.6-0.8 inches,\nyet the northern low will provide the most dynamic support.\nOverall, due to the lack of moisture support by the northern\nsystem, total rainfall amounts will be relatively light, with\naround a tenth to a quarter of an inch of total rainfall, and\nhaving little benefit to the ongoing drought in the area.",
